    "summary": "High‑energy set with standout Playing In The Band and soaring Bird Song",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a tight first set highlighted by Bird Song and a smoking Playing In The Band. The second set maintains the momentum with an inspired Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo into Sugar Magnolia, while Keith’s keyboards add texture throughout.",
    "review": "October 17, 1972 at St. Louis' Fox Theatre finds the Dead firing on all cylinders. From the opening riff of \"The Promised Land\" the band rolls into a crisp, high‑energy first set that peaks with Jerry's soaring lead on \"Playing In The Band\" and a beautifully melodic \"Bird Song\" that showcases his inspired soloing. Phil’s bass is truly on fire, driving the grooves with a fluid intensity that underpins each transition. Keith Godchaux adds shimmering layers on keys, especially during the seamless segue from \"China Cat Sunflower\" into \"I Know You Rider\" and later throughout the set.\n\nThe second set keeps the momentum alive, opening with a heartfelt rendition of \"Greatest Story Ever Told\" before launching into an exuberant \"Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo\" that flows straight into a bright \"Sugar Magnolia\". The encore caps the night with the beloved sing‑along of \"Uncle John's Band\" and a rollicking \"Johnny B. Goode.\" While the recording has a few cuts – notably a mid‑song dropout in \"Black Peter\" and some omitted numbers – the soundboard capture is clear, allowing the band’s tight interplay and dynamic range to shine through.",
